dict(n="Cu Chi tunnels",y=1965,when="Late 1940s (against France) – 1975",where="Near Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam",lane="Southeast Asia",by="Viet Minh and Viet Cong fighters and villagers",group=U,wiki="C%E1%BB%A7_Chi_tunnels",
 mat="Hard laterite clay dug with hoes and baskets",crew="Thousands of villagers over decades",size="Up to about 250 km of tunnels on up to three levels, up to 10 m deep",
 summary="A vast underground network let guerrillas live, hide and launch surprise attacks right next to a major US base during the Vietnam War. Today it is a museum park visited by tourists.",
 steps=["Villagers began digging shelters in the 1940s during the war against France, expanding them in the 1960s.","Clay was dug with short hoes and carried out in baskets, then scattered in bomb craters or rivers so the Americans could not spot fresh soil.","Tunnels were built on several levels, connected by trapdoors, with dead ends and narrow sections to slow pursuers.","Underground spaces included sleeping chambers, kitchens, meeting rooms, weapons workshops, and field hospitals.","'Hoang Cam' kitchens vented smoke through long channels to exit far away, low to the ground, like morning mist.","Entrances were tiny, hidden trapdoors covered by leaves; ventilation holes were disguised as termite mounds."],
 tricks="Tunnels were often too small for most American soldiers. The US sent 'tunnel rats' — volunteers with pistols and torches — and used flooding, gas, dogs and B-52 bombing; fighters disguised air holes with American soap and cigarettes to confuse dogs. Life inside was miserable: darkness, disease, malaria, scorpions and lack of air.",
 legacy="A symbol of Vietnamese resistance and of the limits of US firepower in guerrilla war.",conn="Ho Chi Minh; Cold War; Viet Cong booby traps"),
dict(n="Viet Cong booby traps",y=1965,when="1960s–1975",where="South Vietnam",lane="Southeast Asia",by="Viet Cong guerrillas",group=U,wiki="Booby_trap#Vietnam_War",
 mat="Bamboo, wood, scrap metal, unexploded US bombs",crew="Guerrilla units and villagers",size="Thousands laid across the countryside",
 summary="Cheap, hidden traps such as the punji stick pit were a psychological weapon that slowed and frightened US patrols. Military studies estimate booby traps and mines caused about 11% of US deaths and 17% of wounds.",
 steps=["Punji pits: camouflaged holes lined with sharpened bamboo stakes.","Swinging and falling traps triggered by tripwires on jungle paths.","Grenades and mines made from unexploded American ordnance — the US military estimated much of the explosive in Viet Cong mines came from American duds.","Warning signs known to locals marked trapped areas so villagers could avoid them."],
 tricks="Traps were often designed to wound rather than kill, because a wounded soldier tied up others for evacuation and lowered morale. Many villagers, children and farmers were also injured, and unexploded ordnance still kills people in Vietnam today.",
 legacy="Shaped US jungle training and the image of the Vietnam War as a war without front lines.",conn="Cu Chi tunnels; Cold War"),
dict(n="Iwo Jima tunnel fortress",y=1945,when="1944–1945",where="Iwo Jima, Japan",lane="East Asia",by="Japanese army under General Tadamichi Kuribayashi",group=U,wiki="Battle_of_Iwo_Jima",
 mat="Volcanic rock, concrete",crew="About 21,000 soldiers",size="About 18 km of tunnels and 1,500 rooms",
 summary="Instead of defending the beaches, Kuribayashi turned the tiny volcanic island into an underground fortress, inflicting about 26,000 US casualties.",
 steps=["Soldiers dug tunnels by hand in sulphurous heat up to 50 °C.","Bunkers, pillboxes and gun positions were linked underground.","Mount Suribachi was honeycombed with tunnels on several levels.","Defenders held fire until US Marines landed, then attacked from hidden positions."],
 tricks="Almost all 21,000 defenders died; only 216 were captured. The flag-raising photo on Suribachi became famous.",legacy="One of the bloodiest battles of the Pacific War.",conn="World War II"),
